(1621,3327,'A term used pathologically to describe BILIRUBIN staining of the BASAL GANGLIA; BRAIN STEM; and CEREBELLUM and clinically to describe a syndrome associated with HYPERBILIRUBINEMIA. Clinical features include athetosis, MUSCLE SPASTICITY or hypotonia, impaired vertical gaze, and DEAFNESS. Nonconjugated bilirubin enters the brain and acts as a neurotoxin, often in association with conditions that impair the BLOOD-BRAIN BARRIER (e.g., SEPSIS). This condition occurs primarily in neonates (INFANT, NEWBORN),  but may rarely occur in adults. (Menkes, Textbook of Child Neurology, 5th ed, p613)','en',2,'2026-05-31 02:20:48',NULL,NULL,'11110FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF'),
